margin": -8.44, "analyst_adjustment": -1.56, "key_factors": ["R+8 district with incumbent Hamadeh running for reelection", "Expert consensus of Likely/Solid R across nine raters", "Polymarket prices Democratic chances near 17%", "Large fundraising gap: Hamadeh raised $1.2M vs. $209K for Greene-Placentia"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Republican hold in an R+8 seat, and the expert consensus implies a double-digit Republican margin, so the forecast sits modestly closer to that consensus than the fundamentals alone. Hamadeh's incumbency, fundraising edge, and favorable market pricing leave the Democrat with only a long-shot path.", "watch": "Any late polling showing the race inside single digits, or a major shift in the national environment toward Democrats, would be the clearest sign this is closer than rated.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Abraham Hamadeh (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Abraham Hamadeh is seeking a second full term in Arizona's 8th District, a seat that leans Republican by about eight points. He won renomination in July and enters the general election with a large financial advantage, having raised roughly $1.2 million to about $209,000 for Democratic nominee Bernadette Greene-Placentia. No general-election polling is available for the race.\n\nExpert raters uniformly place the district on the Republican side of the ledger, with several calling it Solid or Safe R and others Likely R. Prediction markets give the Democrat roughly a one-in-six chance. The forecast expects Hamadeh to win by around ten points, with meaningful uncertainty given the absence of polls, keeping the race in Likely Republican territory rather than Safe.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -8.56}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0923, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0824, "margin": -10.0}, {"da